CHAPTER II
PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES
Article 2
(1) The implementation of the protection of women and children is carried out based on the following principles:
a. respect for the rights of women and children;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination in the empowerment of women and children;
d. the best interests of women and children; and
e. restoration of social and economic rights for women and children who experience acts of violence or neglect.
(2) The objectives of implementing the protection of women and children include:
a. preventing acts of violence or neglect against women and children, including trafficking in persons;
b. eliminating all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protecting and providing a sense of security for women and children;
d. providing services to women and children who are victims of acts of violence, as well as reporters and witnesses;
e. facilitating and conducting mediation of domestic disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household; and
f. seeking to obtain damages/compensation for losses suffered by victims from perpetrators of violence.

Article 1 1
(1) The form of prevention as referred to in Article 10 is carried out through:
a. socialization activities of laws and regulations related to the protection of Children and Women to the wider community in the region;
b. activities for disseminating information on the protection of women and children to be known by all people in the region through the distribution of brochures, leaflets, pamphlets or notice boards so that incidents of violence, exploitation and neglect can be reported as soon as possible and can be handled before reaching the most severe condition;
c. training for members who are able to disseminate norms of prohibitions and sanctions to the community;
d. opening of public complaint channels by the Regional Government or Agency through Technical Service Units in each area through community networks;
e. strengthening coordination channels with the Government at the Sub-district, Urban Village/Village level;
f. immediate handling of public complaints throughout all areas in the region and securing subjects of acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking, neglect; and/or people; or
g. counseling/socialization in the form of raising awareness of the wider community down to the smallest community groups (families), especially parents to protect women and children.
(2) The Regional Government through the Agency/UPTD conducts data collection on areas of violence based on incident rates and conducts research and studies on the level of vulnerability of areas to find solutions for reduction up to cessation of acts of violence, exploitation and neglect in vulnerable point areas.

Article 6
The obligations and responsibilities of Regional Government as referred to in Article 5 letter a, include:
a. implementing the obligations for protection of women and children from acts of violence as stipulated by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs and activities for protection of women and children from acts of violence;
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. providing Safe Houses for women and children victims of violence;
f. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with regional financial capacity; and
g. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
h. establishing regional technical implementation units for protection of women and children based on the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 4
Every woman and child who becomes a victim of physical violence, psychological violence, exploitation or human trafficking has the right to receive protection and services.
The protection, assistance, and services for victims as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. to have their dignity respected as human beings;
b. complaints or requests for protection from the concerned party and/or other parties;
c. confidential handling or placement of victims in safe locations and houses whose whereabouts are kept secret to avoid intimidation and threats;
d. medical services/health treatment in the form of care and recovery from injuries or the physical condition of victims by medical personnel and paramedics;
e. medicolegal services for evidence in the legal field;
f. protection by law enforcement officers;
g. provision of information regarding rights and obligations under the law to victims or guardians of victims as well as identification of incidents;
h. continuous handling up to the stage of rehabilitation;
i. recovery of physical, psychological, and sexual health according to the suffering experienced by victims of violence;
j. psychological assistance to restore the traumatic condition of victims and restore the self-confidence of victims;
k. assistance support for legal processes and obtaining the right to compensation or restitution;
l. spiritual guidance services;
m. determining their own decisions;
n. assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
o. guarantee of rights related to status as a family member as well as a member of society (social reintegration); and

CHAPTER IX
COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION
Article 19
(1) In providing protection for women and children, the community may:
a. establish family partners at the kelurahan/desa level by the community;
b. establish women and children protection units within community organizations;
c. conduct independent socialization of women's and children's rights;
d. provide first aid to victims;
and;
e. report to the competent authority if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and mass media.

REGARDING
PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN
BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD
THE REGENT OF BARITO TIMUR,

Considering : a. that women and children with their dignity have the right to be protected from various acts of violence, exploitation, and discrimination that marginalize and degrade their status as human beings;

b. that negative behavior and actions that may result in women and children being in a position of oppression or powerlessness in the social community sphere, or in the legal process sphere should be given special treatment that maintains the stability of their soul and spirit to remain able to carry out their lives in social interaction;

c. that the Regional Government is obligated to provide protection for women and children;

